vibeos (1:0.1.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Built against evolution-data-server 3.62, which is in sid: Contacts,
    Phone, Messages, Calendar and Vibe no longer load EDS 3.56's
    libedataserver beside 3.62's, and 0.1.4's hold of EDS can be lifted.
    The build requires EDS 3.62 now. vCards are written and read through
    3.62's API (e_vcard_convert_to_string, e_vcard_get_attributes_by_name),
    which replaced the calls 3.56 had.
  * Agent is called Vibe where the user sees it: the launcher and the app
    list (a search for Agent still finds it), its window, its settings,
    Settings' Gestures and the package's description. The ids stay
    (vibeos.Agent, vibeos.Dictate, power-hold-agent).
  * Vibe: a power key hold listens on after the key is let go, until the
    speaker pauses, as Android's assistant does after its long press; a
    hold the shell gives up for the power menu sends nothing.
  * Shell: power and volume up open the power menu when pressed as a hand
    does, either key first and held.
  * Shell: a notification's icon is its app's logo in the status bar, on
    the lock screen and on the always-on display, not a white square.
  * Shell: a fullscreen app keeps clear of the camera hole, as on Android.
  * Shell: "Wireless charging" on a charging pad.
  * Shell: vibeos.Shell has the screen's Brightness and ConfirmOwner, the
    owner's PIN or pattern asked for by an app.
  * Keyboard: one closed under a swipe goes, and Back or a touch takes away
    one left on the screen. Console keeps its size and the keys slide in
    over it (vibeos.Shell keyboard-slide-apps, or X-Vibeos-Keyboard=slide
    in an app's desktop file).
  * Video: full screen, by a button beside the menu (f and F11 too), and by
    turning the screen sideways; full screen, a film shot the way the screen
    is turned is cropped to fill it, and one shot the other way stays
    whole.
  * Video: a tap on a control's button no longer hides the controls.
  * Shell: desktop files are read through GioUnix, so GJS no longer warns
    that Gio.DesktopAppInfo has moved.
  * Vibe: the call announcer's autostart entry leaves out
    X-GNOME-Autostart-Phase, which gnome-session warned about at login.
  * Navi: the package's changelog entry is wrapped at 80 columns.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Tue, 24 Nov 2026 04:53:21 +0100

vibeos (1:0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Agent, a new app in vibeos-dictate (vibeos.Agent): a spoken
    conversation with VibeOS's model server, answered in the language
    chosen (English, German, Spanish, French, Italian or Russian) with a
    Piper voice; a language not on the phone is downloaded when it is
    chosen, from pinned addresses with each file's size and SHA-256
    checked.
  * Agent: tools on the phone. It reads the call log, contacts, messages,
    calendar, alarms, battery, storage, the mobile network, Wi-Fi and
    Bluetooth, the weather, the open apps and what was missed; sets and
    cancels alarms and timers, switches Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, opens an app
    and starts a route in Navi. A call, a message, a new contact or an
    event stops at a card and happens only on the user's tap. "What Agent
    can use" switches each kind of data off. What a tool reads is data,
    never an instruction.
  * Agent: a log of what it did (vibeos-dictate/agent-log.jsonl, nothing
    said or read in it) and undo of its last action; in a call it says who
    is calling, and switches the speaker, the microphone and hold, hangs
    up, answers and declines; the core commands work offline in six
    languages; private mode and quiet hours; speech rate, answer length and
    state cues in its settings, a column on a tablet.
  * Holding the power key talks to Agent for as long as it is held, on by
    default (Settings, System, Gestures). On a locked phone Agent only
    reads until the phone is unlocked. The power menu is now power and
    volume up together, and logind leaves the power key's long press to
    the shell (/usr/lib/systemd/logind.conf.d/vibeos-power-key.conf, read at
    the next boot): Mobian's 5 s hold no longer powers the phone off.
  * Gestures: a double press of the power key opens an app (Agent at
    first), and the side button opens any app (Camera at first) or
    recents.
  * Settings: Display size changes the display's scale, and the shell keeps
    it without asking; the volume keys and the quick settings slider go
    above 100% where Settings allows it; the Microphone and Camera switches
    block every app (WirePlumber's permissions for every PipeWire client,
    the Camera app's own check), and the status bar shows them off; High
    contrast in the apps (libvibeos) and the shell.
  * Settings: Set PIN and Set pattern ask for the old one on Settings' own
    screen (vibeos-set-own-password through pkexec, for the active local
    user only). Its PAM service is in /usr/lib/pam.d, which Debian's
    libpam 1.7.0 reads but does not promise to keep reading (#1140029).
  * Settings: an account's "Use for" lists Calendar, Contacts and Files;
    Uninstall goes through App Installer; Default apps offers every app of
    a kind; Tap to wake only with a touchscreen that wakes the screen,
    Vibration only with a motor; an uninstalled app leaves the lists; a
    page shows what changed elsewhere while it is open; GNOME Settings'
    privacy and network panels open the matching page.
  * Files opens folders: the session's default apps
    (/etc/xdg/gnome-mimeapps.list) move from vibeos-installer to
    vibeos-session.
  * Every menu opens below its button (or above it when it does not fit),
    VibeosMenu's and the ones eight apps draw themselves.
  * Tablets: Clock has the rail in both orientations, Contacts' detail
    pane a centred photo and name, Weather's Set City a card, and
    Software's carousel whole columns.
  * Shell: an app can have the keyboard come up over its window
    (X-Vibeos-Keyboard=overlay, vibeos.Shell's KeyboardOverlap);
    vibeos.Shell tells which apps are open and lists the notifications;
    Home minimizes only the phone screen's windows, so a game on a
    plugged-in TV stays open.
  * App Installer has no icon in the launcher.
  * Browser: web videos play and loop, decoded in software (the hardware
    decoder's frames came out black).
  * libvibeos: a sheet's close and a bar's back circle follow the theme, a
    search view can have a button at the bar's end, and a dialog gives
    the focus back only if its answer left it there.
  * Dictate: a language that needs Vosk installs it with its files, and
    Piper 1.5 voices read their vowel clusters as one phoneme.
  * Navi, the car navigation app, is released with this version as a
    package of its own (vibeos-navi, apps/navi/pkg/build-deb.sh).

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 19:40:00 +0100

vibeos (1:0.1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Tablets: the shell and the launcher laid out for a tablet either way up,
    recents as a grid, a floating drawer, the taskbar, two-part quick
    settings tiles, the shade's scrim from the top, and the power and volume
    keys working in recents. The apps lay themselves out for a tablet: two
    panes in Messages, Contacts and Settings, the rail in Software, and
    tablet sizes in Clock, Calculator, Calendar, Phone, Dictate, Browser and
    the Installer.
  * Video Player, a new app (vibeos-video): videos on libmpv under an
    Android player's controls; the default application for videos.
  * Video Player: the ⋮ menu, with the audio and subtitle tracks.
  * Files: thumbnails for videos and images, files open from their icon,
    and a long-press bar and menu with Compress and Batch Rename.
  * Every app has About in its ⋮ menu, and sheets float as cards on a
    tablet.
  * Settings: one app for phones and tablets, in Android's sections and
    names, showing only the rows the device has hardware for; Display in
    Android's sections, Display size & text with a preview. It replaces
    GNOME Settings: whatever opens GNOME Settings opens it at the matching
    page, and installing it removes gnome-control-center. On a tablet it
    says "tablet", and opened from Clock or Contacts it comes to the front.
    Battery information charts the charge since the last full charge, and
    search finds the pages and settings inside the entries. Online
    accounts have pages of their own.
  * Every app takes its colours from the theme.
  * Keyboard: word suggestions in the strip, auto-correction and next-word
    suggestions as Android's keyboard has them (switches in Settings), a
    Tab key on the tablet, and a terminal keeps its screen.
  * Power menu: Android restarts into Android the way the device says
    (/etc/vibeos/android, from the device package).
  * Shade: a phone's shade is Android 17's, with a volume slider below the
    brightness slider.
  * Launcher: draws with OpenGL ES where the GPU has no Vulkan driver, the
    wallpaper redraws only when it moves by a whole pixel, the parallax
    follows the sensor's mount matrix, labels in VibeOS Roboto.
  * The status bar keeps clear of a punch hole in its middle and of the
    camera hole with a display plugged in, and follows only the windows on
    its own screen. Docked, the screen never times out.
  * The screen coming on keeps the brightness it went off with.
  * App sandbox, a new package (vibeos-sandbox): the apps installed from
    Software, or from a .deb file with App Installer, run in a sandbox
    (bubblewrap) with the system read-only, a home of their own, no other
    app's files, no system bus, and only the parts of the session bus their
    permissions allow. The portals ask before an app gets the location or
    the camera, WirePlumber hides the microphones and cameras from an app
    without the permission, and VibeOS's own apps do not run sandboxed.
  * Per-app permissions: Software shows what an app asks for and what an
    app that declares none still gets; Settings, Apps sets an app's network,
    location, camera, microphone, contacts, calendar, phone, SMS, files and
    background permissions, and shows its storage and uninstalls it.
  * Tablets: the settings rows of Phone, Clock, Contacts, Calendar and the
    Browser, Calendar's event editor, App Installer's page and an app's page
    in Software are a column in the middle, so each window turns either way;
    the rail stays beside the pages and the search in Phone, Contacts and
    Software. Clock turns upright from sideways, Calculator's history stays
    open when the tablet is turned, and in a window wider than tall the
    time picker is Android's landscape picker.
  * Settings: Android 17's search bar and names, and Display, Battery, Sound
    and vibration, Connected devices, Apps and Notifications laid out as
    Android 17's; the menu's Battery row says the level, and Apps the apps
    used last.
  * Back closes a dialog with no field; Contacts' editor keeps its fields'
    height and closes at once when nothing changed; Compass says in words
    that the sensor service is missing; Files' More menu has icons, and a
    phone's path bar ends on the folder it shows.
  * Push messages, a new package (vibeos-unifiedpush): a UnifiedPush
    distributor that keeps one connection to push.vibeos.top for all the
    apps, hands each message to its app over D-Bus and starts the app if it
    does not run; messages sent while the phone was offline come once it is
    back. Apps register directly or through libvibeos's VibeosUnifiedPush,
    which decrypts their messages, and sandboxed apps may reach it too.
  * Settings: the main search bar is compact.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 21:45:43 +0100

vibeos (1:0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Software, the new app store: Featured, Apps, Games and Mine, search,
    categories, app pages with screenshots, ratings and reviews, and a
    store account. It replaces GNOME Software on the home screen.
  * Updates: Software checks for new versions in the background and
    suggests each one once, in a notification that opens Mine.
  * vibeos-archive-keyring, a new package: the archive's signing key and
    its apt source, so updates can be installed.
  * App Installer, a new app: installs .deb files and packages from the
    software sources, and uninstalls them. Packages from a signed archive
    install as trusted, and an upgrade asks for nothing but the password.
  * Messages redrawn: the conversation list, a conversation, New chat,
    search and settings.
  * Keyboard: key previews on a short press, panels of several keys that
    stay open, a gear for the settings, and paste.
  * Rotation: an app opens the way the panel is, and a landscape game
    turns the panel as it starts. The heads-up card is centred in
    landscape.
  * Weather: Set City and About. Every app has an About sheet.
  * Battery Saver stays available when power-profiles-daemon starts late,
    and the lock screen no longer throws at every screen off and on.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:24:19 +0100

vibeos (1:0.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Colours: Material 3 colour schemes from a seed colour or the wallpaper,
    light and dark (libtonal, a new library, and vibeos-theme). The apps
    follow vibeos.Theme and the system's colour scheme.
  * Text and display: Font size, Bold text and Display size for the apps
    that ask for them. The screen's scale is used as it is, fractional too,
    and a dp is one logical pixel at any scale.
  * Tablets: two-pane layouts and the first list widgets in libvibeos.
  * Keyboard: VibeOS's own on-screen keyboard, now the default, with the
    AOSP keyboard's layouts and sizes, long press for a key's more keys and
    a tick under the finger. The swipe home works over it, and the keyboard
    goes as soon as the swipe begins. Settings > Keyboard chooses VibeOS's
    keyboard or GNOME's.
  * Contacts: the list screen, Highlights, Organize, and two panes on a
    tablet.
  * Shell: Settings comes to the front from the notification shade and the
    quick settings; a tile's long press opens its own page.
  * Lock screen: a computer's USB port shows as slow charging on any phone.
  * Browser: the tab count is a square with its number centred, in the
    address's font.
  * Text fields: the cursor's handle is in the accent colour and goes away
    as you type.
  * VibeOS icons: Font Awesome Free's phone, comment and camera glyphs for
    Phone, Messages and Camera (CC BY 4.0; the camera white with a black
    lens on dark red), a black circle for the video player, and a console
    icon of its own.
  * Tools: widget dumps, vibeos-gallery and vibeos-specdiff, to measure the
    layouts; vibeos-headless runs without the mutter package.
  * Packaging: new packages libtonal-1-0 and libtonal-dev. The apps depend
    on the exact library version they were built with. The 0.1.0 entry now
    names the relations it used (Breaks/Replaces, not Conflicts/Provides).

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:06:41 +0100

vibeos (1:0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release of VibeOS, the new name of gnome-phone: a GNOME-based
    system for touch devices (phones and tablets now; desktops with touch
    and TV boxes to come). It carries on from gnome-phone 0.1.11; the
    history of gnome-phone's releases is in git.
  * Everything is renamed: the packages (gnome-phone-* and neo-* are
    vibeos-*, libneo is libvibeos, fonts-neo-roboto is fonts-vibeos-roboto),
    the programs, desktop ids, D-Bus names and GSettings schemas (neo.* are
    vibeos.*, neo.Phone is vibeos.Shell), the extension (gnome-phone@neo is
    shell@vibeos), the session (gnome-phone is vibeos), the font family
    (VibeOS Roboto) and the per-user directories.
  * Upgrading from gnome-phone: the vibeos packages replace the old ones
    (Breaks/Replaces). Each old app package (neo-browser, ...)
    is upgraded to an empty transitional package instead of being removed,
    so that the app packages save each user's home layout before the old
    launcher sees an app go and rearranges it. vibeos-session moves the
    users' login session and a mobile shell's unit drop-in over to vibeos,
    and vibeos-migrate-ids carries each user's settings, files, shortcuts and
    home screen over at the next login.
  * Icon packs: VibeOS, the new default (large glyphs on squircles in each
    app's colour), and gnome-free (gnome-phone's Round pack, renamed).
  * Packaging: an upgrade no longer stops the session's units (the phone's
    session is the whole UI); input-boost restarts after an upgrade.
  * The version has an epoch (1:): sim-apn and the libspongy packages keep
    their names, and 1:0.1.0 follows gnome-phone's 0.1.11 for them.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:53:04 +0100
